            <ArticleTitle>Small lipidated anti-obesity compounds derived from neuromedin U.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>616-26</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1016/j.ejmech.2015.07.020</ELocationID>
            <ELocationID EIdType="pii" ValidYN="Y">S0223-5234(15)30152-5</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>A small library of truncated/lipid-conjugated neuromedin U (NmU) analogs was synthesized and tested in vitro using an intracellular calcium signaling assay. The selected, most active analogs were then tested in vivo, and showed potent anorexigenic effects in a diet-induced obese (DIO) mouse model. The most promising compound, NM4-C16 was effective in a once-weekly-dose regimen. Collectively, our findings suggest that short, lipidated analogs of NmU are suitable leads for the development of novel anti-obesity therapeutics.</AbstractText>
